Cyclical Vomiting Syndrome
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Stereotypical recurrent episodes of severe vomiting, Complete resolution between attacks, Predictable periodicity, Normal upper GI endoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-011
Reason for Visit: Severe, unrelenting vomiting with impending clinical dehydration and profound exhaustion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and sibling
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe, continuous vomiting unresponsive to oral antiemetics, posing an immediate risk of hypovolemic shock. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to administer parenteral antiemetics and definitively rule out an acute surgical abdomen prior to the next available in-hours period, preventing an inevitable emergency department admission.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up at 3 AM and haven't stopped throwing up since, I can't even keep a teaspoon of water down."
History of Present Illness: Patient awoke abruptly at 0300 hours with severe nausea, rapidly progressing to profuse, unrelenting vomiting every 15 to 30 minutes. The attack is accompanied by extreme lethargy, severe localized abdominal pain, and excruciating photophobia. This is a highly stereotypical presentation; the patient experiences identical discrete episodes periodically, with a complete return to baseline health between attacks. Simple oral antiemetics were vomited immediately upon ingestion.
Functional Status: Bedbound, positioned directly over an emesis basin, unable to mobilize due to orthostatic dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sibling is providing care but is unable to safely manage or encourage oral fluid intake.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Profoundly pale, lethargic, actively retching, wearing sunglasses indoors due to photophobia.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is completely soft, non-tender, and non-distended with normal active bowel sounds; complete absence of peritonism or palpable masses. Mucous membranes are moderately dry. Capillary refill is 3 seconds. Capillary blood glucose is 5.2 mmol/L. Documented history of normal upper GI endoscopy and metabolic imaging.
Environmental Observations: Room is kept completely dark and quiet; multiple emesis bags filled with bilious fluid are present.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted oral Ondansetron 4mg at home, instantly vomited.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cyclical Vomiting Syndrome - Status: Severe acute exacerbation
Differentials:

Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out due to completely soft, non-distended abdomen, presence of bowel sounds, and lack of prior abdominal surgeries.

Acute Gastroenteritis - Unlikely given the exact stereotypy of the attack, abrupt temporal onset without accompanying diarrhea, and lack of sick contacts.

Diabetic Ketoacidosis - Ruled out due to normal capillary blood glucose and absence of Kussmaul respirations or ketone odor.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with severe, unrelenting cyclical vomiting complicated by impending clinical dehydration and significant orthostatic tachycardia. Oral rehydration and oral antiemetic therapy had completely failed. Immediate parenteral intervention via intramuscular antiemetics and a hands-on clinical examination to definitively exclude an acute surgical abdomen or severe metabolic derangement were mandatory. Delaying this assessment and pharmacological intervention would have rapidly resulted in severe volume depletion, necessitating an emergency department transfer. This encounter strictly satisfies the criteria for an urgent after-hours assessment (MBS Item 597).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ondansetron 8mg stat to break the central emetic cycle. Advised strict bowel rest for 60 minutes post-injection.
Positive Behavioral Change: When the prodromal nausea begins in future episodes, immediately self-administer a prescribed sublingual antiemetic before the actual vomiting starts. Successfully averting the intense vomiting phase will positively reinforce the habit of early, aggressive pharmacological intervention.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to formalize a prophylactic migraine-variant treatment plan (e.g., Amitriptyline or Propranolol).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if vomiting hematemesis (blood or coffee-ground material), becoming completely anuric for 8 hours, or developing sudden severe, constant abdominal pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis relies on the documented exclusion of mechanical bowel issues; any deviation from the stereotypical attack pattern requires immediate reassessment.
Clinical Course: The acute vomiting phase is expected to break within 12 to 24 hours with parenteral support, followed by a profound sleep and a rapid return to completely normal baseline health.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the Emergency Department immediately if you vomit blood, stop producing urine entirely, or if the abdominal pain becomes severe, rigid, and constant.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The intramuscular injection bypasses your gut entirely to directly shut off the vomiting center in your brain, breaking the cycle that oral tablets cannot reach.
Actionable Steps: Wait exactly 60 minutes after the injection, then sip exactly 15ml of oral rehydration fluid every 15 minutes. Tolerating this tiny, strictly measured amount without retching will safely restore hydration and build confidence, reinforcing the paced oral intake.

